    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d295">

                <pb facs="0466" n="1930" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p2/pg1930"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">689.90D/1–551: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Pakistan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Warren</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Karachi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-05T14:00:00+05:30">January 5, 1951—2
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>597. During conversation last night with Mohammad Ali, Secretary General GOP on another subject he gave me to understand
                        GOP plans no further action on our proposal
                    of November 6<anchor n="1" xml:id="d295-f1"/> re discussions between Pakistan
                    and Afghanistan. GOP regards McGhee’s<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d295-f2"/> conversations of November 16 with Zafrullah<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d295-f3"/> and November 27 with Ispahani<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d295-f4"/> as having closed matter so far as it is concerned.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d295-f1" n="1" xml:id="d295-f1-content">
                <p>See the editorial note, <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d295-f2" n="2" xml:id="d295-f2-content">
                <p>George C. McGhee, Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and
                    African Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d295-f3" n="3" xml:id="d295-f3-content">
                <p>Sir Mohammed Zafrulla Khan, Minister of Foreign Affairs and Commonwealth
                    Relations, Government of Pakistan. The conversation of November 16, 1950, was
                    among Zafrulla, Mohammad Ali, and McGhee. This conversation, as well as a
                    further conversation of November 17, in which they were joined by Secretary
                    Acheson, is summarized in telegram 280, to Karachi, November 28, 1950, printed
                    in <ref target="frus1950v05#pg_1457">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1950, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, p. 1457</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d295-f4" n="4" xml:id="d295-f4-content">
                <p>M. A. H. Ispahani, Ambassador of Pakistan in the United States. The conversation
                    of November 27, 1950, is likewise summarized in telegram 280, to Karachi, <ref
                        target="frus1950v05#pg_1457">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>
                    </ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
